A||N||17||David Bartlett||Laser Radar and an angled front plate?||||1||
Z||000000||David Bartlett||11-22-2001||01:11 AM||||Anyone out there experimented with changing the angle of there front plate so Laser Radar can't bounce off right?<P>Does this work? How drastic does the angle have to be?||||reg||1
Z||000001||TylerB11||11-22-2001||01:23 AM||||It all depends...lets take the average distance from the source to be 500 feet. at that distance the beam is over 3 feet wide. Tilting your license plate won't do anything, because the laser should get enough back from your facia to make a distance calculation..ah you poor canucks. you should fight this laser stuff. It doesn't hold up in parts of the US. Its just too damned inaccurate. ||||reg||1
Z||000002||Oreif||11-22-2001||02:31 AM|||| <BLOCKQUOTE><font size="1" face="Verdana, Arial, Helvetica">quote:</font><HR>Originally posted by TylerB11:<BR><B>It all depends...lets take the average distance from the source to be 500 feet. at that distance the beam is over 3 feet wide. Tilting your license plate won't do anything, because the laser should get enough back from your facia to make a distance calculation..ah you poor canucks. you should fight this laser stuff. It doesn't hold up in parts of the US. Its just too damned inaccurate. </B><HR></BLOCKQUOTE><P>It is not 3 feet wide. At 904nm (nano-meters)and the laser beam with a collimated focal length of 40 meters (the lens in the unit) would have an average divergance of 12 microradians per meter. The standard beam is 4mm in diameter. Given a distance of 1000 feet, (maximum range of laser gun, They usually fire it at less than 500 feet) The beam would only be about 22mm. (roughly the size of a quater).<P>As for defeating it by angling the license plate, it won't work. The laser is in the infra-red range of the spectrum so it will travel thru glass and non-opaque plastic. (clear or tinted) When it hits the plate itself or any part of the car, The beam (which is pulsed) will send energy back since it is a collimated beam, The distance of the pulses (PRF) get compressed as the vehicle moves. Time/distance is calculated to a speed. In order to defeat the laser you need to reflect at least 90% of the beams energy away. The only way to do it is to coat the entire car with a coating designed to reflect/diffuse the wavelength of the laser (904nm) then make sure you have nothing to reflect it straight back. There are license plate covers that have a coating to try a diffuse the beam but they have marginal effect. When you try to diffuse a laser beam it scatters. This would give readings that jump around, So he will point it at a different part of the car to get an accurate reading. Now if you put a diffusing coating over the entire car he would never get an accurate speed. It costs my company $250 for a 2" lens coated for a specific laser wavelength in the infra-red range. Wanna take a guess what it would cost to coat an entire car?<P>The return back to the unit is the same principle as your CD player. The beam is reflected back in digital patterns which create the music. The difference with speed laser is they transmit tens to hundreds of pulses per second and count the timing of them. This is all done traveling at the speed of light.<BR><P>------------------<BR>            <IMG SRC="http://www.fiero.nl/uploads/LOGO3.jpg">          <BR><FONT SIZE="1"><B><I>Happiness isn't around the corner...<BR>Happiness IS the corner.</I></B></FONT>||||reg||1
Z||000003||The Fieromaster||11-22-2001||02:43 AM||||Front plate my ass.. I got nailed by laser from the FRONT and i dont have a  front plate... Explain that one!?!<P>------------------<BR>     <IMG SRC="http://www.fiero.nl/forum/smiley_damon.gif">         <FONT COLOR="red">"Raced That, Wrecked Better"</FONT>       <IMG SRC="http://www.fiero.nl/forum/smiley_damon.gif">      <BR>    <IMG SRC="http://www.askfelons.com/fieromaster/fieromastergif1.gif"">                 <BR><B>~In Memory Of Jesse Cesek~ 1980-2000</B><BR><FONT COLOR="blue"><B>AIM: Fieromaster</B></FONT><BR><B><FONT COLOR="purple">1985 GT ~ Purple, Black and Yellow Interior(Fastback Converted)</FONT><BR><FONT COLOR="maroon">1997 Mitsu Eclipse GSX ~ Maroon w/ Hella Balls!(GSX = AWD, Turbo) DOA on 12/10/2000</FONT><BR><FONT COLOR="green">1992 Mitsubishi Eclipse GS ~ soon will be turboed</FONT><BR></B>||||reg||1
