Z||000006||Fierowrecker||11-22-2001||07:52 AM|||| <BLOCKQUOTE><font size="1" face="Verdana, Arial, Helvetica">quote:</font><HR>Originally posted by Oreif:<BR><B> It is not 3 feet wide. At 904nm (nano-meters)and the laser beam with a collimated focal length of 40 meters (the lens in the unit) would have an average divergance of 12 microradians per meter. The standard beam is 4mm in diameter. Given a distance of 1000 feet, (maximum range of laser gun, They usually fire it at less than 500 feet) The beam would only be about 22mm. (roughly the size of a quater).<P>As for defeating it by angling the license plate, it won't work. The laser is in the infra-red range of the spectrum so it will travel thru glass and non-opaque plastic. (clear or tinted) When it hits the plate itself or any part of the car, The beam (which is pulsed) will send energy back since it is a collimated beam, The distance of the pulses (PRF) get compressed as the vehicle moves. Time/distance is calculated to a speed. In order to defeat the laser you need to reflect at least 90% of the beams energy away. The only way to do it is to coat the entire car with a coating designed to reflect/diffuse the wavelength of the laser (904nm) then make sure you have nothing to reflect it straight back. There are license plate covers that have a coating to try a diffuse the beam but they have marginal effect. When you try to diffuse a laser beam it scatters. This would give readings that jump around, So he will point it at a different part of the car to get an accurate reading. Now if you put a diffusing coating over the entire car he would never get an accurate speed. It costs my company $250 for a 2" lens coated for a specific laser wavelength in the infra-red range. Wanna take a guess what it would cost to coat an entire car?<P>The return back to the unit is the same principle as your CD player. The beam is reflected back in digital patterns which create the music. The difference with speed laser is they transmit tens to hundreds of pulses per second and count the timing of them. This is all done traveling at the speed of light.<P><BR></B><HR></BLOCKQUOTE><P>Hey Orief!<BR>All he needs is black hole paint...<BR>Sucks in all the light and doesn't allow any to escape!<BR>crash...||||reg||1
Z||000007||johnt671||11-22-2001||07:56 AM||||Read a car and driver story about laser a few years ago.  They said that any shiny fairly flat spot is a good aiming point.  If I remember right they were able to come close to beating it with a Firebird and some kind of tape all over the front.  A front plate makes it easier for them.  ||||reg||1
Z||000008||Oreif||11-22-2001||09:25 AM|||| <BLOCKQUOTE><font size="1" face="Verdana, Arial, Helvetica">quote:</font><HR>Originally posted by The Fieromaster:<BR><B>Front plate my ass.. I got nailed by laser from the FRONT and i dont have a  front plate... Explain that one!?!<P></B><HR></BLOCKQUOTE><P>Any flat part on the front of the car will give a return. The bumpers, parking lights, headlights, etc. He can even target the backside of the rear view mirror and get a reading. Any part of the car that is flat forward and the size of a quater will work.<BR>||||reg||1
Z||000009||avengador1||11-22-2001||09:34 AM||||I had read that driving with the high beams on would reduce the effective range of the laser gun. Is this true or another urban legend? and if it's true why does it work?<P>------------------<BR> <IMG SRC="http://www.fiero.nl/uploads/Avendgador1.jpg"> ||||reg||1
Z||000010||Formula||11-22-2001||09:35 AM||||what if you kick it down a gear and outrun the laser||||reg||1
Z||000011||pherder||11-22-2001||09:47 AM||||I remember the article that johnt671 wrote about.<P>They taped over the turn signal lenses with black electrical tape and made sure there wasn't anything "reflective" showing (probably removing the rear view mirror, etc.)<P>The Firebird was black and they mounted a large spotlight in front and put a lens over it so that it emitted only in the infra-red range.  The stated that they couldn't get a lock on the car until it was <B>REAL</B> close (I believe &lt; 50 feet) which should have been enough time for the driver to slow down.<P>All this is from memory ... it has been up to 10 years ago (or more) that I read this and they are professionals so don't try this at home!  <IMG SRC="http://www.fiero.nl/forum/wink.gif">  I do remember that I thought that the Fiero would have been an even better car to try this with so I had to be since 1983 when I read this since I have owned Fieros since they came out on the market.  <IMG SRC="http://www.fiero.nl/forum/biggrin.gif"><P>Paul<BR>(Owner of a BRAND NEW 1984 Fiero Coupe in 1983!)||||reg||1
Z||000012||Oreif||11-22-2001||10:04 AM||||  <BLOCKQUOTE><font size="1" face="Verdana, Arial, Helvetica">quote:</font><HR>Originally posted by Formula:<BR><B>what if you kick it down a gear and outrun the laser</B><HR></BLOCKQUOTE><P>Your Fiero can go faster than the speed of light when you kick it down a gear?   <IMG SRC="http://www.fiero.nl/forum/eek.gif"><P>High beams are an urban legend. Although they do emit the wavelength, it is very small when compared to a specific collimated beam.<BR>The deal with the Firebird and the spotlight set-up for IR range could work given the spotlight had enough power to saturate the detecting diode. You could even blow out the diode if you could fire a laser of the same wavelength but at a greater power than the limits of the detector. But you would need a system to track and target the laser gun in order to hit the detecting diode directly.<p>[This message has been edited by Oreif (edited 11-22-2001).]||||reg||1
Z||000013||87FieroGTx||11-22-2001||12:00 PM|||| <BLOCKQUOTE><font size="1" face="Verdana, Arial, Helvetica">quote:</font><HR>Originally posted by Formula:<BR><B>what if you kick it down a gear and outrun the laser</B><HR></BLOCKQUOTE><P><BR> Yah.. my car will also do 669,600,000 +1 mph, won't yours?<P>||||reg||4
